import Scaffold from '../components/Scaffold.astro';
---
<Base title="Approach — Paul Hendrie" description="How I think about learning design, scaffolding, noticing, and what AI is actually for.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="kicker">Approach</p>
    <h1 class="settle">Support that plans its own retreat</h1>

    <Scaffold>
      Reading guide: this essay makes four claims — about scaffolding,
      noticing, whose language counts, and AI. The margin notes are where I
      argue with myself. (This box will quietly leave once you're underway.
      That's claim one, enacted.)
    </Scaffold>

    <p>Most of what I believe about learning design fits in one sentence:
    <mark>good support is support that plans its own retreat</mark>. A
    scaffold on a building is not the building; everyone involved knows it
    comes down, and its coming down is not a failure of the scaffold but the
    point of it. Teaching that never withdraws isn't generous — it's a
    dependency with a friendly face.</p>

    <Note>
      Vygotsky is the usual citation here, but the builder's metaphor does
      more honest work than the ZPD diagram. Nobody photographs the
      scaffolding.
    </Note>

    <p>This sounds obvious and is quietly radical, because almost every
    incentive in education pushes the other way. Support that stays visible
    gets counted, funded and thanked. Support that fades looks, from the
    outside, like doing less. A well-designed course, tool or tutorial is
    always working toward the moment it isn't needed — which means the
    measure of my work is not what learners do with it, but what they can
    do <em>after</em> it, without it.</p>

    <h2 class="settle">Noticing over telling</h2>

    <p>English for Academic Purposes taught me the second conviction:
    <mark>noticing beats telling</mark>. You can tell a student that
    academic writers hedge their claims, and the information will slide off.
    Or you can put two sentences in front of them — <em>this causes
    that</em> and <em>this appears to contribute to that</em> — and ask what
    changed and why a careful writer might want the second. The knowledge a
    learner builds by noticing is theirs; the knowledge they were told is
    still yours, on loan.</p>

    <Note>
      There's a one-minute working demonstration of this on the
      <a href="/notice/">Notice</a> page. It will teach you more about my
      practice than this paragraph can — which is, of course, the claim.
    </Note>

    <p>Most of my design decisions are downstream of this. It's why my
    activities lead with authentic text rather than rules, why my AI coach
    asks questions instead of issuing corrections, and why this website
    keeps writing you margin notes instead of announcing its qualities in a
    hero banner.</p>

    <h2 class="settle">In-sessional, not remedial</h2>

    <p>In-sessional EAP — the support that runs alongside students' actual
    degree courses — carries a stigma it doesn't deserve, because it's so
    often framed as remediation: fixing deficient English until its owners
    resemble native speakers. I think that framing is wrong twice.
    Academic English is <mark>nobody's first language</mark>; it's a set of
    disciplinary conventions that everyone, everywhere, has to be let in on.
    And the conventions live inside the disciplines — the hedging that makes
    a claim careful in history overclaims in engineering. So the support
    belongs inside the disciplines too: reading the texts students actually
    read, in the week they actually read them.</p>

    <Note>
      The academic literacies tradition (Lea &amp; Street) makes this
      argument properly. My version: if your writing support could belong to
      any course anywhere, it belongs to none.
    </Note>

    <h2 class="settle">What AI is actually for</h2>

    <p>Which brings me to the question I now get asked most. I use AI
    constantly in my design work, and the reason isn't efficiency — it's
    that <mark>AI collapses the cost of making the right thing</mark>.
    The gap between a good learning design and the design that actually
    ships has always been labour: the walkable museum you can't build alone,
    the fifty-five activities that each need bespoke flawed texts, the coach
    who's available at 11pm. AI moves those from "worth doing, can't afford
    it" to "shipped".</p>

    <p>But the same property makes AI dangerous in exactly one way: it also
    collapses the cost of making the <em>wrong</em> thing, at scale, with
    confidence. A model will cheerfully produce the answer machine that does
    the student's noticing for them — the anti-scaffold, support that never
    retreats because retreat was never designed in. So my rule is boring and
    absolute: <mark>the pedagogy decides, the model executes</mark>. Every
    stance in this essay — fading support, noticing over telling, no
    deficit framing — was encoded into my tools as constraints before any
    student met them. When I can't state the pedagogic constraint precisely
    enough to encode it, that's not a limitation of the tooling. That's the
    design work I haven't finished yet.</p>

    <Note>
      This is also why "prompt engineering" undersells the job. Writing a
      good system prompt for a tutor is curriculum design in a new genre —
      the hard part was never the prompt.
    </Note>

    <p>None of this is a manifesto against telling, or lectures, or answer
    keys, or dark mode. It's a preference order, and like every preference
    order it costs something: designs that fade are harder to measure, and
    noticing is slower than telling. I think the trade is right. The essays
    my students write after the support has gone are the only evidence I
    fully trust.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
